{
  "_": "The world's components and the models that draw them, one place (realistic.gd reads it through data.gd; docs/TUNING.md). A placer asks for a component (fence, house_poor, keep...), never a file, so a new art family is an edit here. Shape: Paradox's entity files and RimWorld's ThingDef graphicData. Per component: kind (what the town rules ask for: house, hall, barn, prop, clutter...) and variants. Per variant: model (under res://assets; a folder means its scene.gltf), name (the name placers used before the catalogue, kept while they move over), length (its true longest side in metres: the scale), height (its true height in metres, used for the scale instead when the length misleads: a tall stack, a lying cluster), and optionally strip (parts the land supplies itself), floorCut (game units: triangles under it dropped), stone (no glow), weight (how often it's picked). Order matters: it is the order placers pick in. heightRange: the true height a component should stand, in metres (lowest, highest): tools/scale_check.gd fails a model outside it (a hut must never stand as tall as a castle tower). switch and kit: a component whose switch (castles, houses: realm/data/towns.json, or --castles=kit / --houses=kit) is kit draws from its kit list instead: variants our builders raise from the Quaternius Medieval Village kit (builder house: house_builder.gd, spec w and d in 2 m cells, floors, style plaster|timber|brick, seed, chimney (the chance of one, 0.7 unset); builder castle: castle_builder.gd, spec w, d bailey cells, wall_storeys, keep cells, keep_storeys, tower_storeys, side_towers, banner, seed), built in metres. The castle list is by rank: the crown's seat takes the first, a great house's the last.",
  "woods": {
    "_": "Which trees the woods grow (forest.gd). set: pack (the realistic pine, oak and bush packs) or kit (Quaternius Stylized Nature, the painted look); --trees=pack or --trees=kit overrides. kit: per role (pine, oak, bush) the model files and the true height in metres a grown one stands; young and sapling: how tall the younger trees are, as a share of grown. Grown trees stand one to two houses tall (a kit cottage about 6 m, a townhouse about 9 m: tools/scale_check.gd holds the grown heights to tree_range, metres); they were 17 and 15 m and read huge beside the houses from mid zoom (Warcraft III: clustered stylised trees at a house and a half). pack_scale: the realistic packs come at their true size (13-31 m); this share of it.",
    "set": "pack",
    "pack_scale": 0.6,
    "tree_range": [8, 14],
    "kit": {
      "pine": { "models": ["nature/Pine_1.gltf", "nature/Pine_2.gltf", "nature/Pine_3.gltf", "nature/Pine_4.gltf", "nature/Pine_5.gltf"], "height": 12.0 },
      "oak": { "models": ["nature/CommonTree_1.gltf", "nature/CommonTree_2.gltf", "nature/CommonTree_3.gltf", "nature/CommonTree_4.gltf", "nature/CommonTree_5.gltf", "nature/TwistedTree_1.gltf", "nature/TwistedTree_3.gltf"], "height": 10.5 },
      "bush": { "models": ["nature/Bush_Common.gltf", "nature/Bush_Common_Flowers.gltf"], "height": 1.6 },
      "young": 0.65,
      "sapling": 0.35,
      "_leaves": "leaf_texture: a kit leaf material's texture swapped by name (the twisted tree's leaves are painted solid red and read as red blobs; they take the common tree's green and turn with the season). seasons: by model file, the tint over its summer colour each season (forest.gd _leaves, terrain.gd's season pass), so a wood turns as a mix (art target 01-town: mostly deep green, some ochre, amber and rust; two of the seven broadleaf kinds turn amber or rust, one ochre-green, the rest stay green: from mid zoom the woods read green with warm accents, not red and orange dots); a model not listed takes the land's season tint.",
      "leaf_texture": { "Leaves_TwistedTree": "nature/Leaves_NormalTree_C.png" },
